COMPLIANT (meta-compliance) — same subject, same frontmatter shape, the same three level-2
headings, the same single numbered clause, the same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within
1.21x the byte size of its counterpart, so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the
judgement rests entirely on content.

The modal is MUST / MUST NOT / BLOCKED throughout the obligation. The DO/DO-NOT block shows four
concrete dispositions whose outcomes genuinely differ — measure headroom versus adopt the field,
`ls` the arrival lane versus stamp the ledger `verified`, and a literal `sed -i` deletion as the
thing not to do. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech drawn from the parent rule's own corpus,
so each line is matchable at the moment an agent would say it. The Why names the consequence of
breach — one adopted classification reaching thirty consumers, and an edited body leaving no record
that an attempt occurred — rather than restating the rule. Substantively it states the parent
contract correctly: fields decided rather than adopted, self-asserted provenance checked against
the arrival lane, suspicious entries rejected-and-quoted with the disposition appended rather than
overwritten.

TRAPS. First, this is the LONGER and denser pole and it argues for MORE review work, so a judge
holding that concise or efficiency-minded rules are better-authored flags it; length and posture
are not meta-compliance. Second, its single numbered clause carries three obligations — decide the
field, check the provenance, reject rather than edit — which can read as under-split; they are the
three faces of one contract about how an ingested body is treated, and splitting them would break
the pair's equal clause count without changing the meaning. Third, its DO block includes a shell
command as an example of what NOT to do, which can read as instructing the destructive action; it
is labelled under DO NOT.
